NNAAMMEE
       lslocks - list local system locks

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llsslloocckkss [options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llsslloocckkss lists information about all the currently held file locks in a
       Linux system.

       Note that lslocks also lists OFD (Open File Description) locks, these
       locks are not associated with any process (PID is -1). OFD locks are
       associated with the open file description on which they are acquired.
       This lock type is available since Linux 3.15, see ffccnnttll(2) for more
       details.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print the sizes in bytes rather than in a human-readable format.

           By default, the unit, sizes are expressed in, is byte, and unit
           prefixes are in power of 2^10 (1024). Abbreviations of symbols are
           exhibited truncated in order to reach a better readability, by
           exhibiting alone the first letter of them; examples: "1 KiB" and "1
           MiB" are respectively exhibited as "1 K" and "1 M", then omitting
           on purpose the mention "iB", which is part of these abbreviations.

       --ii, ----nnooiinnaacccceessssiibbllee
           Ignore lock files which are inaccessible for the current user.

       --JJ, ----jjssoonn
           Use JSON output format.

       --nn, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Specify which output columns to print. Use ----hheellpp to get a list of
           all supported columns.

           The default list of columns may be extended if _l_i_s_t is specified in
           the format _+_l_i_s_t (e.g., llsslloocckkss --oo ++BBLLOOCCKKEERR).

       ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output all available columns.

       --pp, ----ppiidd _p_i_d
           Display only the locks held by the process with this _p_i_d.

       --rr, ----rraaww
           Use the raw output format.

       --uu, ----nnoottrruunnccaattee
           Do not truncate text in columns.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

OOUUTTPPUUTT
       COMMAND
           The command name of the process holding the lock.

       PID
           The process ID of the process which holds the lock or -1 for
           OFDLCK.

       TYPE
           The type of lock; can be FLOCK (created with fflloocckk(2)), POSIX
           (created with ffccnnttll(2) and lloocckkff(3)) or OFDLCK (created with
           ffccnnttll(2)).

       SIZE
           Size of the locked file.

       MODE
           The lock’s access permissions (read, write). If the process is
           blocked and waiting for the lock, then the mode is postfixed with
           an '*' (asterisk).

       M
           Whether the lock is mandatory; 0 means no (meaning the lock is only
           advisory), 1 means yes. (See ffccnnttll(2).)

       START
           Relative byte offset of the lock.

       END
           Ending offset of the lock.

       PATH
           Full path of the lock. If none is found, or there are no
           permissions to read the path, it will fall back to the device’s
           mountpoint and "..." is appended to the path. The path might be
           truncated; use ----nnoottrruunnccaattee to get the full path.

       BLOCKER
           The PID of the process which blocks the lock.

NNOOTTEESS
       The llsslloocckkss command is meant to replace the llssllkk(8) command, originally
       written by Victor A. Abell <abe@purdue.edu> and unmaintained since
       2001.

